\end{vmatrix}
\\
&\quad + \left(-\frac{9}{2}\right)
\begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & \frac{11}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{7}{3}
\end{vmatrix}
- \frac{13}{3}
\begin{vmatrix}
-\frac{13}{3} & \frac{25}{6} & -\frac{19}{6} \\
-\frac{23}{6} & \frac{29}{6} & -\frac{10}{3} \\
-\frac{17}{6} & -\frac{7}{6} & \frac{5}{2}
\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]

That's a lot of computation, but let's see if there's a shortcut. Let's try adding all the rows together:

Add up all the rows:

First column:  
\(\frac{4}{3} + (-\frac{13}{3}) + (-\frac{23}{6}) + (-\frac{17}{6}) = \frac{4 - 13}{3} + \frac{-23 - 17}{6}\)  
\(\frac{-9}{3} + \frac{-40}{6} = -3 + (-\frac{20}{3}) = -\frac{29}{3}\)

Second column:  
\(1 + \frac{25}{6} + \frac{29}{6} + (-\frac{7}{6}) = 1 + \frac{25 + 29 - 7}{6} = 1 + \frac{47}{6} = \frac{6 + 47}{6} = \frac{53}{6}\)

Third column:  
\(-\frac{9}{2} + (-\frac{19}{6}) + (-\frac{10}{3}) + \frac{5}{2}\)  
\(-\frac{9}{2} + \frac{5}{2} = -2\)  
\(-\frac{19}{6} - \frac{10}{3} = -\frac{19}{6} - \frac{20}{6} = -\frac{39}{6} = -\frac{13}{2}\)  
So sum: \(-2 - \frac{13}{2} = -2 - 6.5 = -8.5 = -\frac{17}{2}\)

Fourth column:  
\(-\frac{13}{3} + \frac{11}{6} + (-\frac{19}{6}) + \frac{7}{3}\)  
\(-\frac{13}{3} + \frac{7}{3} = -2\)  
\(\frac{11}{6} - \frac{19}{6} = -\frac{8}{6} = -\frac{4}{3}\)  
Sum: \(-2 - \frac{4}{3} = -\frac{6}{3} - \frac{4}{3} = -\frac{10}{3}\)

So the total is:
\[
(-\frac{29}{3},\ \frac{53}{6},\ -\frac{17}{2},\ -\frac{10}{3})
\]
None of the columns add to zero. Let's try another property.

Let's try to see if the rows are linearly dependent.

Let's try to write the last row as a combination of others.
Let \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) such that
\[
a \cdot \text{Row}_1 +
b \cdot \text{Row}_2 +
c \cdot \text{Row}_3 = \text{Row}_4
\]

Let's try \(a = 1, b = 1, c = 1\):

First entry: \(\frac{4}{3} + (-\frac{13}{3}) + (-\frac{23}{6}) = -3 - \frac{23}{6} = -\frac{18}{6} - \frac{23}{6} = -\frac{41}{6}\). This is not \(-\frac{17}{6}\).

